            <td class="lt-chem-459840 mt-align-center" data-align="left" data-th="General formula" data-valign="top"><span class="unnumbered" data-alt="The structure of lactam, a cyclic amide, in which the alkyl group from one side of the carbonyl is bonded through a cyclic structure to the nitrogen on the other side." data-type="media"><img alt="The structure of lactam, a cyclic amide, in which the alkyl group from one side of the carbonyl is bonded through a cyclic structure to the nitrogen on the other side." data-height="126" data-media-type="image/jpeg" data-width="152" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/459247/f14d05f53910d1791e3aa7aee9b2ae49c8b9dbfe?revision=1" /></span></td>
            <td class="lt-chem-459840 mt-align-center" data-align="left" data-th="Name ending" data-valign="top">None</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<p class="lt-chem-459840">It&rsquo;s useful to classify carbonyl compounds into two categories based on the kinds of chemistry they undergo. In one category are aldehydes and ketones; in the other are carboxylic acids and their derivatives. The acyl group in an aldehyde or ketone is bonded to an atom (H or C, respectively) that can&rsquo;t stabilize a negative charge and therefore can&rsquo;t act as a leaving group in a nucleophilic substitution reaction. The acyl group in a carboxylic acid or its derivative, however, is bonded to an atom (oxygen, halogen, sulfur, nitrogen) that <em data-effect="italics">can</em> stabilize a negative charge and therefore <em data-effect="italics">can</em> act as a leaving group in a nucleophilic substitution reaction.</p>

<figure><img alt="Aldehydes and ketones lack appropriate leaving groups for nucleophilic substitution, whereas compounds like carboxylic acids, acid halides, esters, thioesters, amides, acid anhydrides, and acyl phosphates have suitable leaving groups." data-height="570" data-media-type="image/jpeg" data-width="977" style="width: 763px; height: 445px;" width="763px" height="445px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/459248/23c8e0bf7e365411965b784641b6f7eee2d2ae77?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=763&amp;height=445" /></figure>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_(OpenStax)/18:_Ethers_and_Epoxides_Thiols_and_Sulfides/18.14:_Preview_of_Carbonyl_Chemistry" class="mt-section" id="section_2"><span id="II_Nature_of_the_Carbonyl_Group"></span><h2 class="lt-chem-459840 editable">II Nature of the Carbonyl Group</h2>

<p class="lt-chem-459840">The carbon&ndash;oxygen double bond of a carbonyl group is similar in many respects to the carbon&ndash;carbon double bond of an alkene. The carbonyl carbon atom is <em data-effect="italics">sp</em><sup>2</sup>-hybridized and forms three <em data-effect="italics">&sigma;</em> bonds. The fourth valence electron remains in a carbon <em data-effect="italics">p</em> orbital and forms a \(\pi\) bond to oxygen by overlapping with an oxygen <em data-effect="italics">p</em> orbital. The oxygen atom also has two nonbonding pairs of electrons, which occupy its remaining two orbitals.</p>

<figure><img alt="The orbital representation of carbonyl group and alkene. The electrostatic potential map demonstrates the likeness between the carbonyl group's double bonds and highlights nonbonding electron pairs on the oxygen atom." data-height="254" data-media-type="image/jpeg" data-width="589" style="width: 477px; height: 206px;" width="477px" height="206px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/459251/aafbddd28075cd775e24f34f86fd529382238bf3?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=477&amp;height=206" /></figure>

<p class="lt-chem-459840">Like alkenes, carbonyl compounds are planar about the double bond and have bond angles of approximately 120&deg;. Figure \(\PageIndex{1}\) shows the structure of acetaldehyde and indicates its bond lengths and angles. As you might expect, the carbon&ndash;oxygen double bond is both shorter (122 pm versus 143 pm) and stronger [732 kJ/mol (175 kcal/mol) versus 385 kJ/mol (92 kcal/mol)] than a C&ndash;O single bond.</p>

<figure><img alt="The structure and ball-and-stick model in the electrostatic potential map of acetaldehyde. Values of bond lengths and bond angles are mentioned. Electron-rich and electron-poor atoms in the model are labeled." data-height="321" data-media-type="image/jpeg" data-width="1026" style="width: 769px; height: 241px;" width="769px" height="241px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/459250/ea0e2dc13b9a0d08c2d6bba95a82bdbed43b21ac?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=769&amp;height=241" />
<figcaption>Figure \(\PageIndex{1}\): Structure of acetaldehyde.</figcaption>
</figure>

<p class="lt-chem-459840">As indicated by the electrostatic potential map in Figure \(\PageIndex{1}\), the carbon&ndash;oxygen double bond is strongly polarized because of the high electronegativity of oxygen relative to carbon. Thus, the carbonyl carbon atom carries a partial positive charge, is an electrophilic (Lewis acidic) site, and reacts with nucleophiles. Conversely, the carbonyl oxygen atom carries a partial negative charge, is a nucleophilic (Lewis basic) site, and reacts with electrophiles. We&rsquo;ll see in the next five chapters that the majority of carbonyl-group reactions can be rationalized by simple polarity arguments.</p>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_(OpenStax)/18:_Ethers_and_Epoxides_Thiols_and_Sulfides/18.14:_Preview_of_Carbonyl_Chemistry" class="mt-section" id="section_3"><span id="III_General_Reactions_of_Carbonyl_Compounds"></span><h2 class="lt-chem-459840 editable">III General Reactions of Carbonyl Compounds</h2>

<p class="lt-chem-459840">Both in the laboratory and in living organisms, most reactions of carbonyl compounds take place by one of four general mechanisms: <em data-effect="italics">nucleophilic addition, nucleophilic acyl substitution, alpha substitution,</em> and <em data-effect="italics">carbonyl condensation.</em> These mechanisms have many variations, just as alkene electrophilic addition reactions and S<sub>N</sub>2 reactions do, but the variations are much easier to learn when the fundamental features of the mechanisms are made clear. Let&rsquo;s see what the four mechanisms are and what kinds of chemistry carbonyl compounds undergo.</p>

<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_(OpenStax)/18:_Ethers_and_Epoxides_Thiols_and_Sulfides/18.14:_Preview_of_Carbonyl_Chemistry" class="mt-section" id="section_4"><span id="Nucleophilic_Addition_Reactions_of_Aldehydes_and_Ketones_(Chapter_19)"></span><h3 class="lt-chem-459840 editable">Nucleophilic Addition Reactions of Aldehydes and Ketones (Chapter 19)</h3>

<p class="lt-chem-459840">The most common reaction of aldehydes and ketones is the <strong>nucleophilic addition reaction</strong>, in which a nucleophile, <strong>:</strong>Nu<sup>&ndash;</sup>, adds to the electrophilic carbon of the carbonyl group. Because the nucleophile uses an electron pair to form a new bond to carbon, two electrons from the carbon&ndash;oxygen double bond must move toward the electronegative oxygen atom to give an alkoxide anion. The carbonyl carbon rehybridizes from <em data-effect="italics">sp</em><sup>2</sup> to <em data-effect="italics">sp</em><sup>3</sup> during the reaction, and the alkoxide ion product therefore has tetrahedral geometry.</p>

<figure><img alt="The reversible reactions show the formation of a tetrahedral intermediate (s p 3 hybridized) formed from the attack of a nucleophile on the carbonyl compound (s p 2 hybridized)." data-height="201" data-media-type="image/jpeg" data-width="598" style="width: 492px; height: 165px;" width="492px" height="165px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/459252/ef1c226ddcad3cda3e4d7a413fa2be5b962a0f6c?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=492&amp;height=165" /></figure>

<p class="lt-chem-459840">Once formed, and depending on the nature of the nucleophile, the tetrahedral alkoxide intermediate can undergo one of two further reactions, as shown in Figure \(\PageIndex{2}\). Often, the tetrahedral alkoxide intermediate is simply protonated by water or acid to form an alcohol product. Alternatively, the tetrahedral intermediate can be protonated and expel the oxygen to form a new double bond between the carbonyl carbon and the nucleophile. We&rsquo;ll study both processes in detail in Chapter 19.</p>

<figure><img alt="Aldehyde or ketone addition to nucleophile yields either an alcohol or a C double bonded to N u product, based on the nucleophile." data-height="347" data-media-type="image/jpeg" data-width="957" style="width: 785px; height: 285px;" width="785px" height="285px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/459260/23fa5ae668dda936afe71fd644274a2280846c22?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=785&amp;height=285" />
<figcaption>Figure \(\PageIndex{2}\): The addition reaction of an aldehyde or a ketone with a nucleophile. Depending on the nucleophile, either an alcohol or a compound with a <math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>C</mtext><mtext>&#9552;</mtext></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Nu</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><mrow><mtext>C</mtext></mrow></annotation-xml><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><mrow><mtext>&#9552;</mtext><mtext>Nu</mtext></mrow></annotation-xml></semantics></math> double bond is formed.</figcaption>
</figure>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_(OpenStax)/18:_Ethers_and_Epoxides_Thiols_and_Sulfides/18.14:_Preview_of_Carbonyl_Chemistry" class="mt-section" id="section_5"><span id="FORMATION_OF_AN_ALCOHOL"></span><h3 class="lt-chem-459840 editable">FORMATION OF AN ALCOHOL</h3>

<p class="lt-chem-459840">The simplest reaction of a tetrahedral alkoxide intermediate is protonation to yield an alcohol. We&rsquo;ve already seen two examples of this kind of process during reduction of aldehydes and ketones with hydride reagents such as NaBH<sub>4</sub> and LiAlH<sub>4</sub> (Section 17.4) and during Grignard reactions (Section 17.5). During a reduction, the nucleophile that adds to the carbonyl group is a hydride ion, \(\ce{H:^{&ndash;}}\), while during a Grignard reaction, the nucleophile is a carbanion, \(\ce{R3C:^{&ndash;}}\).</p>

<figure><img alt="Reduction reaction involves ketones or aldehydes converting to alcohols. The Grignard reaction involves the conversion of ketones or aldehydes to alcohols. Both reactions progress via a tetrahedral intermediate." data-height="482" data-media-type="image/jpeg" data-width="786" style="width: 678px; height: 416px;" width="678px" height="416px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/459257/79936d73f3120bc63f7da5d97831fbec978eaf2b?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=678&amp;height=416" /></figure>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_(OpenStax)/18:_Ethers_and_Epoxides_Thiols_and_Sulfides/18.14:_Preview_of_Carbonyl_Chemistry" class="mt-section" id="section_6"><span id="FORMATION_OF_C.3DNu"></span><h3 class="lt-chem-459840 editable">FORMATION OF C=Nu</h3>

<p class="lt-chem-459840">The second mode of nucleophilic addition, which often occurs with amine nucleophiles, involves elimination of oxygen and formation of a \(\ce{C=Nu}\) double bond. For example, aldehydes and ketones react with primary amines, \(\ce{RNH2}\), to form imines, \(\ce{R2C=NR'}\). These reactions use the same kind of tetrahedral intermediate as that formed during hydride reduction and Grignard reaction, but the initially formed alkoxide ion is not isolated. Instead, it is protonated and then loses water to form an imine, as shown in Figure \(\PageIndex{3}\).</p>

<figure><img alt="Mechanism of reaction of an amine with aldehyde or ketone; attack produces tetrahedral structure, protonation of oxygen follows, loss of O H minus gives neutral imine." data-height="829" data-media-type="image/jpeg" data-width="971" style="width: 683px; height: 584px;" width="683px" height="584px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/459259/6de799b3f2102355ed31c39275f3ea4c96f02a92?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=683&amp;height=584" />
<figcaption>Figure \(\PageIndex{3}\): Formation of an imine, \(\ce{R2C=NR&prime;}\), by reaction of an amine with an aldehyde or a ketone.</figcaption>
</figure>
</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_(OpenStax)/18:_Ethers_and_Epoxides_Thiols_and_Sulfides/18.14:_Preview_of_Carbonyl_Chemistry" class="mt-section" id="section_7"><span id="Nucleophilic_Acyl_Substitution_Reactions_of_Carboxylic_Acid_Derivatives_(Chapter_21)"></span><h3 class="lt-chem-459840 editable">Nucleophilic Acyl Substitution Reactions of Carboxylic Acid Derivatives (Chapter 21)</h3>

<p class="lt-chem-459840">The second fundamental reaction of carbonyl compounds, <strong>nucleophilic acyl substitution</strong>, is related to the nucleophilic addition reaction just discussed but occurs only with carboxylic acid derivatives rather than with aldehydes and ketones. When the carbonyl group of a carboxylic acid derivative reacts with a nucleophile, addition occurs in the usual way, but the initially formed tetrahedral alkoxide intermediate is not isolated. Because carboxylic acid derivatives have a leaving group bonded to the carbonyl-group carbon, the tetrahedral intermediate can react further by expelling the leaving group and forming a new carbonyl compound:</p>

<figure><img alt="A carboxylic acid derivative reaction involving nucleophile addition that leads to a tetrahedral intermediate, which then eliminates the leaving group to form a new carbonyl compound." data-height="314" data-media-type="image/jpeg" data-width="737" style="width: 570px; height: 243px;" width="570px" height="243px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/459261/e769a76e24b93233e886fe88d50dc7ef092d3c9e?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=570&amp;height=243" /></figure>

<p class="lt-chem-459840">The net effect of nucleophilic acyl substitution is the replacement of the leaving group by the entering nucleophile. We&rsquo;ll see in Chapter 21, for instance, that acid chlorides are rapidly converted into esters by treatment with alkoxide ions (Figure \(\PageIndex{4}\)).</p>

<figure><img alt="An alkoxide ion adds to an acid chloride, forming a tetrahedral intermediate, which then leads to the substitution of a chloride ion with oxygen, resulting in an ester product." data-height="664" data-media-type="image/jpeg" data-width="971" style="width: 767px; height: 524px;" width="767px" height="524px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/459263/9dc2a4092cf0e2e38c15dca30c1194c3d2e66139?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=767&amp;height=524" />
<figcaption>Figure \(\PageIndex{4}\): Nucleophilic acyl substitution of an acid chloride with an alkoxide ion yields an ester.</figcaption>
</figure>
</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_(OpenStax)/18:_Ethers_and_Epoxides_Thiols_and_Sulfides/18.14:_Preview_of_Carbonyl_Chemistry" class="mt-section" id="section_8"><span id="Alpha-Substitution_Reactions_(Chapter_22)"></span><h3 class="lt-chem-459840 editable">Alpha-Substitution Reactions (Chapter 22)</h3>

<p class="lt-chem-459840">The third major reaction of carbonyl compounds, <strong>alpha substitution</strong>, occurs at the position next to the carbonyl group&mdash;the alpha (<em data-effect="italics">&alpha;</em>) position. This reaction results in the substitution of an <em data-effect="italics">&alpha;</em> hydrogen by an electrophile through the formation of an intermediate <em data-effect="italics">enol</em> or <em data-effect="italics">enolate ion:</em></p>

<figure><img alt="A carbonyl with an alpha hydrogen is shown in equilibrium with enol or enolate; either of these can then react with an electrophile to produce an alpha-substituted carbonyl." data-height="422" data-media-type="image/jpeg" data-width="672" style="width: 534px; height: 335px;" width="534px" height="335px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/459269/bed6a55bb7faca36e9166e2c715f1aac6c97b3c6?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=534&amp;height=335" /></figure>

<p class="lt-chem-459840">For reasons that we&rsquo;ll explore in Chapter 22, the presence of a carbonyl group renders the hydrogens on the <em data-effect="italics">&alpha;</em> carbon acidic. Carbonyl compounds therefore react with strong base to yield enolate ions.</p>

<figure><img alt="Base abstracts the alpha proton on a carbonyl to produce a carbanion, which is resonance-stabilized by formation of enolate." data-height="220" data-media-type="image/jpeg" data-width="779" style="width: 604px; height: 171px;" width="604px" height="171px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/459268/ab78f56fbcf8f4f8a53956b69d77219a67672fae?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=604&amp;height=171" /></figure>

<p class="lt-chem-459840">Because they&rsquo;re negatively charged, enolate ions act as nucleophiles and undergo many of the reactions we&rsquo;ve already studied. For example, enolates react with primary alkyl halides in the S<sub>N</sub>2 reaction. The nucleophilic enolate ion displaces halide ion, and a new C&ndash;C bond forms:</p>

<figure><img alt="Base abstracts alpha proton of carbonyl to produce carbanion, shown in resonance with enolate. Enolate attacks R C H 2 X via S N 2 to produce alpha substituted carbonyl." data-height="219" data-media-type="image/jpeg" data-width="879" style="width: 704px; height: 175px;" width="704px" height="175px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/459270/f69b938690b6859621804423db9a6ded8eb5053a?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=704&amp;height=175" /></figure>

<p class="lt-chem-459840">The S<sub>N</sub>2 alkylation reaction between an enolate ion and an alkyl halide is a powerful method for making C&ndash;C bonds, thereby building up larger molecules from smaller precursors. We&rsquo;ll study the alkylation of many kinds of carbonyl compounds in Chapter 22.</p>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_(OpenStax)/18:_Ethers_and_Epoxides_Thiols_and_Sulfides/18.14:_Preview_of_Carbonyl_Chemistry" class="mt-section" id="section_9"><span id="Carbonyl_Condensation_Reactions_(Chapter_23)"></span><h3 class="lt-chem-459840 editable">Carbonyl Condensation Reactions (Chapter 23)</h3>

<p class="lt-chem-459840">The fourth and last fundamental reaction of carbonyl groups, <strong>carbonyl condensation</strong>, takes place when two carbonyl compounds react with each other. When acetaldehyde is treated with base, for instance, two molecules combine to yield the hydroxy aldehyde product known as <em data-effect="italics">aldol</em> (<em data-effect="italics">ald</em>ehyde + alcoh<em data-effect="italics">ol</em>):</p>

<figure><img alt="Two equivalents of acetaldehyde in sodium hydroxide produce 3-hydroxybutanal, an aldol." data-height="182" data-media-type="image/jpeg" data-width="661" style="width: 544px; height: 150px;" width="544px" height="150px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/459271/9a8b5bbabcbf1ceb194d9ddf8ee6fc414f235047?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=544&amp;height=150" /></figure>

<p class="lt-chem-459840">Although carbonyl condensation appears to be different from the three processes already discussed, it&rsquo;s actually quite similar. A carbonyl condensation reaction is simply a combination of a nucleophilic addition step and an <em data-effect="italics">&alpha;</em>-substitution step. The initially formed enolate ion of one acetaldehyde molecule acts as a nucleophile and adds to the carbonyl group of another acetaldehyde molecule, as shown in Figure \(\PageIndex{5}\).</p>

<figure><img alt="Mechanism of enolate formation: hydroxide abstracts alpha proton from acetaldehyde, carbanion attacks carbonyl of another acetaldehyde, protonation generates neutral 3-hydroxybutanal product." data-height="1013" data-media-type="image/jpeg" data-width="978" style="width: 741px; height: 768px;" width="741px" height="768px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/459273/148db667d71a65e7c5be01ce74e445d3fa435e8d?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=741&amp;height=768" />
<figcaption>Figure \(\PageIndex{5}\): A carbonyl condensation reaction between two molecules of acetaldehyde yields a hydroxy aldehyde product.</figcaption>
</figure>
</div></div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_(OpenStax)/18:_Ethers_and_Epoxides_Thiols_and_Sulfides/18.14:_Preview_of_Carbonyl_Chemistry" class="mt-section" id="section_10"><span id="IV_Summary"></span><h2 class="lt-chem-459840 editable">IV Summary</h2>

<p class="lt-chem-459840">To a great extent, the chemistry of living organisms is the chemistry of carbonyl compounds. We have not looked at the details of specific carbonyl reactions in this short preview but rather have laid the groundwork for the next five chapters. All the carbonyl-group reactions we&rsquo;ll be studying in Chapters 19 through 23 fall into one of the four fundamental categories discussed in this preview. Knowing where we&rsquo;ll be heading should help you keep matters straight in understanding this most important of all functional groups.</p>

<section class="box-exercise">
<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_(OpenStax)/18:_Ethers_and_Epoxides_Thiols_and_Sulfides/18.14:_Preview_of_Carbonyl_Chemistry" class="mt-section"><span id="Exercise_.5C(.5CPageIndex.7B1.7D.5C)"></span><h5 class="box-legend editable"><span class="lt-icon-default">Exercise \(\PageIndex{1}\)</span></h5>

